--TEST--
Core\Db::connect names the field that makes a `[db.<name>]` block unopenable, and a granted name with no block at all is its own answer — `rule:core-classes/db-connection-is-named`, `rule:core-classes/db-capabilities` and `rule:core-classes/db-column-types`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Every name below is granted, so every refusal here is about the file rather
// than about the grant. The six blocks are the ways one can fail before a
// socket is ever opened, and each one names the field that is wrong -- an
// operator reading these lines edits one line of `nvs.toml` and no more.
array<string> $names = ["absent", "mariadb", "mssql", "mysql", "sqlite", "unusable", "zone"];
foreach ($names as string $name) {
    try {
        var $db = Core\Db::connect($name);
        echo $name, ": opened", "\n";
    } catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
        echo $refused->message, "\n";
    }
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
# `absent` is granted and has no block: the grant names a block an operator has
# not written, which is a different mistake from an unwritten grant and gets a
# different sentence.
[capabilities.db]
connect = ["absent", "mariadb", "mssql", "mysql", "sqlite", "unusable", "zone"]

# A third driver this build *does* open, read by its own resolver and refused on
# the same first field MySQL's is. MariaDB is not a MySQL flag -- `rule:core-classes/db-connection-is-named` --
# so it is its own reader answering here and not MySQL's with a switch in it.
[db.mariadb]
driver = "mariadb"
host = "127.0.0.1"

# A fourth driver this build *does* open, refused on the same first field the
# other three are. SQL Server reads the block through its own resolver -- ADR
# 0067 § 2's `driver` again -- and a `password` is what LOGIN7 cannot be built
# without, so the sentence is its own and not a stand-in for a missing driver.
[db.mssql]
driver = "mssql"
host = "127.0.0.1"

# A driver this build *does* open, so the block is read by MySQL's own resolver
# and the field it names is the first one the handshake needs -- `rule:core-classes/db-connection-is-named`'s
# `driver` deciding which reader the rest of the block goes through.
[db.mysql]
driver = "mysql"
host = "127.0.0.1"

# A block that resolves as its own driver's and then fails at the file, which is
# where every other block on this list fails too: the refusal is the engine's
# and names the path, so a `sqlite` block reads as configured-and-unreachable
# rather than as a driver nothing here supports -- `rule:core-classes/db-connection-is-named` and `rule:core-classes/db-capabilities`. The
# directory cannot exist on any host this suite runs on, which is what makes the
# sentence the same one everywhere.
[db.sqlite]
driver = "sqlite"
path = "/no-such-directory-for-this-case/novis.db"

# `path` is SQLite's field. A PostgreSQL connection reads nothing from it, and
# a field silently ignored is how a block comes to mean something nobody wrote.
[db.unusable]
driver = "postgres"
host = "127.0.0.1"
user = "novis"
password = "novis"
database = "novis"
path = "/var/lib/novis.db"

# `rule:core-classes/db-column-types` sends the declared zone to the server as a numeric offset, so a
# zone *name* is refused rather than read as UTC -- silently reading it as UTC
# would put every zone-less `TIMESTAMP` an hour or more out.
[db.zone]
driver = "postgres"
host = "127.0.0.1"
user = "novis"
password = "novis"
database = "novis"
time_zone = "Europe/Vienna"
--EXPECT--
Core\Db::connect: nothing sets `[db.absent]` up, so the name resolves to a block an operator has not written yet
Core\Db::connect: [db.mariadb]: the block names no `password`, which a MariaDB connection cannot be opened without
Core\Db::connect: [db.mssql]: the block names no `password`, which a SQL Server connection cannot be opened without
Core\Db::connect: [db.mysql]: the block names no `password`, which a MySQL connection cannot be opened without
Core\Db::connect: `[db.sqlite]` at `/no-such-directory-for-this-case/novis.db` did not open: sqlite ERROR: unable to open database file: /no-such-directory-for-this-case/novis.db
Core\Db::connect: [db.unusable]: `path` belongs to another driver, and a PostgreSQL connection reads nothing from it
Core\Db::connect: [db.zone]: `time_zone` is `Europe/Vienna`, which is not an offset: write `+02:00`, `-05:30` or `UTC`, or leave it unset for UTC
